  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "There are NNN boxes. Initially, each box contains one ball.\n\nFor each KKK from 111 to NNN, solve the following problem:\n\nChoose a subset of boxes of size KKK equiprobably at random, and place one additional ball in each chosen box.\n\nThen again choose a subset of boxes of size KKK equiprobably at random, and place one additional ball in each chosen box. This second choice is independent of the first.\n\nWhat is the expected value of the product of the number of balls over all boxes? It can be shown that the answer can be expressed as PQ\\frac{P}{Q}QP​ for some integers P,QP, QP,Q, where QQQ is not divisible by 998 244 353998\\,244\\,353998244353. Output such value 0≤R&lt;998 244 3530 \\le R &lt; 998\\,244\\,3530≤R&lt;998244353, that P≡QR mod 998 244 353P \\equiv QR \\bmod 998\\,244\\,353P≡QRmod998244353.\n\n\nInput Format\nThe only line of input contains one integer NNN.\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nPrint NNN numbers, the KKK-th of them is the expected value of the product for parameter KKK modulo 998 244 353998\\,244\\,353998244353.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤N≤250 0001 \\le N \\le 250\\,0001≤N≤250000"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3",
        "output": "665496239 11 27",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nIf K=1K=1K=1, with probability 1/31/31/3 one box will have 3 balls and two boxes will have 1 ball, and with probability 2/32/32/3 two boxes will have 2 balls and one box will have 1 ball. Thus the answer is 1/3⋅(3⋅1⋅1)+2/3⋅(2⋅2⋅1)=11/31/3 \\cdot (3 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1) + 2/3 \\cdot (2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1) = 11/31/3⋅(3⋅1⋅1)+2/3⋅(2⋅2⋅1)=11/3.\nIf K=2K=2K=2, with probability 1/31/31/3 two boxes will have 3 balls and one box will have 1 ball, and with probability 2/32/32/3 two boxes will have 2 balls and one box will have 3 balls. Thus the answer is 1/3⋅(3⋅3⋅1)+2/3⋅(2⋅2⋅3)=111/3 \\cdot (3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 1) + 2/3 \\cdot (2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3) = 111/3⋅(3⋅3⋅1)+2/3⋅(2⋅2⋅3)=11.\nIf K=3K=3K=3, with probability 1 all three boxes will have 3 balls, thus the answer is 1⋅(3⋅3⋅3)=271 \\cdot (3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3) = 271⋅(3⋅3⋅3)=27."
      }
    ]
  },
